# A Node harness for browser-built workers

<p class="lede">anon-rpc's Node harness runs a hash-pinned worker inside a sandbox where it starts with nothing and every capability is a host grant. Atmos forks it so that a worker built for the browser, such as the Nym worker, can run there too.</p>

## Why fork it

The upstream harness runs workers in a QuickJS interpreter compiled to WebAssembly. QuickJS has no WebAssembly engine of its own, and the Nym worker is a 10 MB WebAssembly client that also opens a WebSocket to its entry gateway. Neither is available in the upstream sandbox, so the Nym worker cannot start there.

## What changed

| | Upstream | Atmos fork |
|---|---|---|
| Sandbox | QuickJS, interpreted | a separate V8 isolate (isolated-vm) |
| WebAssembly | none | V8's own; the promise forms are built on the synchronous ones |
| Guest JavaScript speed | about 50× slower than V8 | V8 speed |
| `fetch` | granted, host-performed, address policy | unchanged |
| WebSocket | none | granted (`webSocket`), host-opened under the same address policy |
| Browser platform pieces | none | granted (`web`): base64, Blob URLs, an in-sandbox Worker and MessageChannel, Request, events, DOMException |
| Bundle | fetched from the resolvers each boot | optional cache by hash, rechecked on every load |

The guest still starts with nothing: no `process`, no `require`, no module loader, no network of its own. `web` adds only pure computation. `webSocket` and `fetch` are the only ways out, and both go through the host's address policy.

## How it is tested

- **Upstream's own suite** passes unchanged on the V8 sandbox. It checks the realm escape, the missing ambient platform, the address policy, and hostile workers: an infinite loop, deep recursion and heap exhaustion. All three are contained, and the host survives.
- **A live Nym boot** (`test/nym.mjs`) loads the hash-pinned worker, connects through the mixnet and answers JSON-RPC calls.

## Nym on the Node harness

The live, hash-pinned Nym worker boots in the forked harness with no browser. In one run it connected to an exit router in 1.3 seconds, was ready at 18.9 seconds, and answered `eth_blockNumber` through the mixnet in about 0.6 seconds. The [self-hosted endpoint](/docs/self-host/) runs on it by default: a 5-minute wallet-style session answered all 73 calls, 69 of them proof-checked.

Getting there took one fix that matters to anyone porting a browser worker. The Nym client sends its binary frames as `Blob` objects, so a WebSocket grant must send a Blob's bytes, not read it like a typed array.

## Use it

```js
import { AnonRpcWorker } from "./harness/node-harness/dist/host.js";

const worker = new AnonRpcWorker({
  address: "0x3aCD9742C68F6d993066Ae25533e09b9e1f60eD0", // Nym worker, mainnet specifier
  preExisting: { rpcProvider },
  capabilities: { fetch: true, webSocket: true, web: true },
  limits: { memoryBytes: 1024 ** 3, deadlineMs: 60_000 },
  bundleCacheDir: ".bundles",
});
await worker.ready;
```

`createAtmosClient` takes the class as its `Worker` option, so the Atmos client runs on either harness unchanged.
